Step 1: Create the Project
Create a folder
mkdir express-learning
cd express-learning
Initialize Node.js
npm init -y

This creates:

package.json
Install Express
npm install express
Step 2: Create the Entry File

Create:

server.js

Question:

What does a server need first?

Answer:
A server needs to:

Listen on a port
Accept requests

Write this:

const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log("Server running on port 3000");
});
Run it
node server.js

You should see:

Server running on port 3000
Understanding
const app = express();

Creates an Express application.

app.listen(...)

Starts the server.

Think of it as:

Express App
    ↓
Listens on Port 3000
    ↓
Ready for Requests
Step 3: Create Your First Route

A route decides:

If someone visits this URL, what should happen?

Add:

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
  res.send("Hello Express");
});

Full code:

const express = require("express");

const app = express();

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
  res.send("Hello Express");
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log("Server running on port 3000");
});
Test

Open:

http://localhost:3000

Output:

Hello Express
Understand
app.get()

Means:

GET request
URL path
Callback
app.get("/", callback)

When user visits:

/

Run the callback.

Step 4: Learn Request and Response

Look at:

(req, res)

These are the most important objects in Express.

Request Object (req)

Contains information coming from the client.

Example:

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
  console.log(req.method);
  console.log(req.url);

  res.send("Check terminal");
});

Visit:

localhost:3000

Terminal:

GET
/

Useful request properties:

req.method
req.url
req.params
req.query
req.body
req.headers

Don't memorize them now.

Just remember:

req = incoming data
Response Object (res)

Used to send data back.

Examples:

Send text
res.send("Hello");
Send JSON
res.json({
  name: "John"
});
Set status
res.status(404).send("Not Found");

Remember:

res = outgoing data
Step 5: Multiple Routes

Add:

app.get("/", (req, res) => {
  res.send("Home");
});

app.get("/about", (req, res) => {
  res.send("About Page");
});

app.get("/contact", (req, res) => {
  res.send("Contact Page");
});

Now:

/
/about
/contact

all work separately.

Step 6: Route Parameters

Suppose:

/user/1
/user/2
/user/3

Instead of creating many routes:

app.get("/user/:id", (req, res) => {
  res.send(req.params.id);
});

Visit:

/user/100

Output:

100
Understand
:id

is a placeholder.

Express stores it in:

req.params

Example:

console.log(req.params);

Output:

{
  id: "100"
}
Step 7: Query Parameters

URL:

/products?category=laptop

Route:

app.get("/products", (req, res) => {
  res.send(req.query.category);
});

Output:

laptop

Difference:

/user/10
       ↑
params

/products?category=laptop
                     ↑
query
Step 8: Middleware

Before a route runs, Express can execute some code.

That's middleware.

Create:

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log("Middleware executed");

  next();
});
Full Flow
Request
   ↓
Middleware
   ↓
Route
   ↓
Response
Why next()?

Without:

next();

the request gets stuck.

Middleware
   ↓
STOP

With:

next();
Middleware
   ↓
Next Middleware/Route
Step 9: Create a Custom Middleware

Example:

function logger(req, res, next) {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);

  next();
}

app.use(logger);

Every request gets logged.

Example:

GET /
GET /about
GET /user/10
Step 10: Handle POST Requests

Install body parser support (built into Express):

app.use(express.json());

Add route:

app.post("/users", (req, res) => {
  console.log(req.body);

  res.send("User created");
});

Test with Postman:

{
  "name": "John"
}

Output:

{
  name: "John"
}
Step 11: Error Handling

Normal middleware:

(req, res, next)

Error middleware:

(err, req, res, next)

Notice:

err

comes first.

Example:

app.get("/error", (req, res) => {
  throw new Error("Something broke");
});

Add at the bottom:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.log(err.message);

  res.status(500).json({
    error: err.message
  });
});

Visit:

/error

Response:

{
  "error": "Something broke"
}
